id="rubric">
             The estimation report: five criteria, four levels each, scored by the rule above. The report meets the bar at level 3 or above on every criterion. 
               Criterion  4 · Exemplary  3 · Proficient  2 · Developing  1 · Beginning   
             
                C1 · Instrument design  Everything in level 3, and lists the pairs with prizes in cents and exact probabilities, and shows the mixed-sign pairs.  States the number of pairs (at least 12), the prize scale in cents, and why the pairs contain gains and losses, and gives two example pairs with cents and exact probabilities.  States the number of pairs and the prize scale and gives the reason for the losses, with no example pair.  No instrument described: no count of pairs, no prize scale, and no pair shown.  
                C2 · Estimation model  Everything in level 3, and names the simplification (no probability weighting), and states which pairs identify loss aversion and which identify precision.  States the value function, the logit choice rule, and the likelihood with its term for each response: log P(A) for a response that chose A, and log(1-P(A)) for one that chose B.  States the value function and the logit rule, but the likelihood does not give the per-response term.  No model stated, or only one of the value function and the logit rule.  
                C3 · Uncertainty  Everything in level 3, and states that the standard errors are from the observed information at the estimate, names the sample size, and says when the standard errors would be unreliable.  Everything in level 2, and the convergence check, the sample size and the asymptotic assumption.  Reports the three estimates with  se_gamma ,  se_lambda  and  se_kappa .  No standard error reported, or a number is quoted that the run does not print.  
                C4 · Interpretation and limits  Everything in level 3, and for each of at least two limits states the direction its error would take, for example, which way the curvature estimate would move if the participant's choices were noisier than the logit rule assumes.  Says what the estimates do and do not say about this participant, names at least two limits (one participant, hypothetical stakes, a small sample, the omitted probability weighting), and says that a standard error larger than its estimate means the parameter is not pinned down.  Names one limit.  No limits stated, or the estimates are treated as facts about the participant beyond the questionnaire.  
                C5 · Evidence  Everything in level 3, and every claim carries a grade and a key or a derivation, and no locator is stated that was not read.  Every empirical claim carries a grade and a registry key, or is marked as a computed result of Lab 3, and no locator is invented.  At least one empirical claim has a grade and a registry key, and at least one lacks one.  No empirical claim has a grade or a registry key.  
             
           
         

        
=====EXEMPLAR A + B MEMO TEXT=====
id="exemplar-a" data-level="2" data-scores="2,2,1,2,1" data-label="Exemplar A" aria-labelledby="exemplar-a-title">
 Exemplar A 
 [verdict removed]
 
 
 Instrument 
 The questionnaire has twelve pairs of lotteries paid in dollars. Some of the lotteries lose money, because the estimate of loss aversion needs losses. 
 
 [annotation removed]
 
 
 
 Model and estimates 
 The value function is \(v(x)=x^{1-\gamma}\) for gains and \(v(x)=-\lambda(-x)^{1-\gamma}\) for losses, with curvature \(\gamma\) and loss weight \(\lambda\). The choices follow the logit rule \(P(A)=1/(1+e^{-z})\), with \(z=\kappa(\mathrm{CE}_A-\mathrm{CE}_B)/10\,000\) and precision \(\kappa\). The likelihood is the sum of the log probabilities of the choices. The estimates are a curvature of 0.6352, a loss aversion of 6.1503 and a precision of 2.0201. 
 
 [annotation removed]
 
 
 
 Uncertainty 
 The estimates are the three values above, and no standard error is given. 
 
 [annotation removed]
 
 
 
 Limitations and evidence 
 The answers are from one participant. Loss aversion is found in most people. 
 
 [annotation removed]
 
 

 
 Exemplar B 
 [verdict removed]
 
 
 Instrument 
 The questionnaire has twelve pairs. The prizes are whole cents between \(-18\,700\) and \(19\,000\), and each lottery has two or three prizes with probabilities such as \(9/10\) and \(1/10\) that sum to 1 exactly. Pair 0 is a lottery of \(6200\) cents with probability \(9/10\) and \(-15100\) cents with probability \(1/10\), against three prizes in B. Pair 3 sets 18800 cents with probability 3/4 and \(-12500\) cents with probability 1/4 against 16500 cents with probability 2/5 and \(-17700\) cents with probability 3/5; both lotteries mix a gain and a loss. The pairs include losses because a lottery whose positive-probability prizes include no gain, or include no loss, has a certainty equivalent that does not depend on \(\lambda\), so a pair identifies loss aversion only when one of its lotteries mixes a gain and a loss, as the Identification convention shows. Eleven of the twelve pairs contain a mixed lottery (Lab 3 prints this count), and the estimate refuses any set of answers with no mixed lottery. 
 
 [annotation removed]
 
 
 
 Model and likelihood 
 The value function is \(v(x)=x^{1-\gamma}\) for gains and \(-\lambda(-x)^{1-\gamma}\) for losses, with \(0 
 
 [annotation removed]
 
 
 
 Estimates and uncertainty 
 The estimates are gamma_hat = 0.6352, lambda_hat = 6.1503 and kappa_hat = 2.0201, with standard errors 0.32, 9.59 and 1.86, from the observed information at the estimate. The largest score component at the estimate is 4.00e-10 (Lab 3), which is the convergence check. The sample has 12 answers, so the asymptotic approximation may be poor. The standard error of \(\hat\lambda\) is larger than \(\hat\lambda\), so these answers do not pin loss aversion down. The log-likelihood at the estimate is \(-7.1445\). 
 
 [annotation removed]
 
 
 
 Interpretation and limits 
 The estimates describe this participant's answers to these twelve pairs. Loss aversion is not pinned down: its standard error exceeds the estimate. The answers are SIMULATED and the stakes are hypothetical, and the model has no probability weighting. I expect, without computing it here, that noisy choices and curvature are hard to tell apart when the pairs are close; that is a hypothesis for you to test. I expect, without computing it here, that a design with more close pairs would raise the standard error of curvature; that is a hypothesis for you to test. 
 
 [annotation removed]
 
 
 
 Evidence 
 Every estimate, standard error, count and score component above is a computed result of Lab 3, graded C with basis "Lab 3 (SIMULATED)". The prize range and the number of prizes in each lottery are read from the data that Lab 3 prints, and are marked the same way. The value function and the choice rule are stated as definitions on this page. The memo cites no published result, so it attributes nothing to the registry sources; the reading section lists them for the lessons. 
 
 [annotation removed]
 
 

 
